 {"name": "A metaphor that needs no receiver", "who": "Gregory of Nazianzus; John of Damascus; Anselm; most theologians today", "view": "The ransom tells us that we were captive, that freedom cost a life, and that God paid it, but no one received a payment. Asking 'to whom?' presses a picture beyond what it was meant to say.", "evidence": "Oration 45.22; the New Testament's silence about a receiver; Anselm, Why God Became Man I.7."},
 {"name": "A price paid to God's justice", "who": "Many Reformed theologians, following Calvin", "view": "There is a receiver, but it is not the devil: it is the justice of God, which must be satisfied if sin is to be forgiven. The ransom picture points towards the courtroom.", "evidence": "Calvin speaks of Christ 'ransoming us from the justice of God' (Institutes II.16.12); Romans 3:24–26."}
